  "response": "The most likely cause is repeated wall-clock reads inside `src/billing.ts`: seven direct `Date.now()` / `new Date()` calls, several within the 204-line `generateInvoice`.\n\nIf invoice generation crosses midnight between those calls, different parts of one invoice can observe different dates. That produces classic boundary-only flakiness. The large function makes this hidden temporal coupling harder to trace, while the weak billing tests fail to protect the expected date behavior.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n```ts\ntype Clock = {\n  now(): Date;\n};\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(),\n};\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  input: InvoiceInput,\n  clock: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const now = clock.now(); // capture exactly once\n  // Pass `now` to every helper and calculation.\n}\n```\n\nThen add boundary tests with a fixed clock, covering just before/after midnight and the intended timezone:\n\n```ts\nconst clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(\"2026-12-31T23:59:59.999Z\"),\n};\n\nconst invoice = generateInvoice(input, clock);\n\nexpect(invoice.billingDate).toBe(\"2026-12-31\");\n```\n\nAlso standardize billing calculations on UTC or an explicitly selected business timezone. If `billing.ts` calls helpers from `src/date.ts`, fix those too: the scan reports mixed UTC/local methods, timezone-less parsing, and DST-naive day arithmetic there. Avoid a broad refactor initially—inject and capture the clock, add strong boundary assertions, then extract the date calculation from `generateInvoice` into a pure helper.",
